BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SML
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261105T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261105T200000
UID:5bb7db7f1ed637efb0480973aa789ce8@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:IU Jazz Studies\, Jacobs School of Music\, International Anthe
 m Record Company\, and Visit Bloomington Present: SML\nA quintet composed 
 of luminaries from Los Angeles’s thriving jazz\, improvised\, and indie 
 music scenes: bassist Anna Butterss (Jeff Parker\, Jason Isbell\, Phoebe B
 ridgers\, Makaya McCraven)\, synthesist Jeremiah Chiu (Ariel Kalma\, Marta
  Sofia Honer)\, saxophonist Josh Johnson (Meshell Ndegeocello\, Flea\, Leo
 n Bridges\, Carlos Niño)\, percussionist Booker Stardrum (Wendy Eisenberg
 \, Horse Lords\, Nels Cline) and guitarist Gregory Uhlmann (Sam Wilkes\, M
 eg Duffy\, Perfume Genius).\nTheir debut album Small Medium Large was reco
 rded at the band’s first gigs at Highland Park venue ETA in late 2022 an
 d early 2023\, post-produced in the following year\, and released in Summe
 r 2024 to wide critical acclaim\, landing on ‘Best of 2024’ lists by T
 one Glow\, Passion of the Weiss\, Bandcamp Daily\, Hearing Things\, and ot
 hers. Following the release of Small Medium Large\, the band quickly expan
 ded beyond Los Angeles\, performing at various festivals and venues and sh
 arpening their sound. Recordings from these shows were then used as the ba
 sis for their sophomore album HOW YOU BEEN. This second release showcases 
 a road-tested band pushing razor sharp confidence in the form of trance-in
 ducing rhythm focused improvisation\, but it also delves even deeper into 
 the possibilities of post-production.\nUpon its late-2025 release\, HOW YO
 U BEEN was thoroughly praised by critics and fans alike\, landing on numer
 ous best of the year lists and even catching the attention of the New York
  Times\, who published an in-depth feature on the band. The live shows tha
 t followed the album release had a similarly wide appeal\, and support fro
 m well-credentialed outlets like NPR’s World Cafe have bolstered the fer
 vent energy which has seen SML’s name recognition grow so quickly.\nIn M
 arch of 2026 SML held a residency at the Big Ears Festival\, where the ban
 d played 6 sets over 3 nights to hundreds of fans\, many of whom attended 
 every show. The full\, unedited SML experience is clearly of interest. In 
 that spirit\, Spontaneous Music Live—an album of two unedited sidelong i
 mprovisations recorded at Zebulon in LA—was released in summer 2026\, of
 fering the first official recorded glimpse into the psychedelic realism an
 d linear\, hypnotic expansion of SML’s live sound.\nThere will be a 2nd 
 set with SML XL featuring John Raymond & Greg Ward.
GEO:39.1662844;-86.5328085
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E. Kirkwood Avenue\, Bloomington\, 
 IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/sml/59895/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Treasures of the Silk Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261105T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261105T180000
UID:ffde9f818164c666c38f17d20be00817@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The McCalla Center\, in collaboration with musician and schola
 r Shahyar Daneshgar\, celebrates the opening of its new exhibition Treasur
 es of the Silk Road on Friday\, September 25th\, 4:00 pm-6:00 pm EDT.\nThe
  exhibit features over 70 musical instruments from the Pastel-Daneshgar mu
 sical instrument collection\, with a focus on strings\, flutes\, and percu
 ssion from across Asia\, the Middle East\, and North Africa. Crafted from 
 wood\, snakeskin\, horsehair\, lambskin\, camel bone\, and other unique ma
 terials\, these instruments provide a compelling history of cultural and a
 rtistic exchanges that have led to the myriad musical sounds and styles th
 at permeate the world today. The opening event will feature remarks from t
 he collection owner\, Shahyar Daneshgar\, and will feature musical clips p
 rovided by the Lotus World Music & Arts Festival.\nShahyar Daneshgar is a 
 lifelong musician\, scholar\, and cultural ambassador whose passion for tr
 aditional music has shaped more than three decades of collecting\, teachin
 g\, and performing. Born into a large musical family\, he studied at the T
 ehran Conservatory of Music before pursuing academic work in Germany and l
 ater at Indiana University\, where he earned his Ph.D. in Central Eurasian
  Studies in 1995. He taught at Indiana University until his retirement in 
 2024. He is founder and director of the Bloomington Silk Road Ensemble.\nT
 he exhibition is free and runs from September 25\, 2026\, to September 202
 7.\nThe mission of the McCalla Center is to provide accessible exhibition 
 and educational experiences\, creating opportunities for students\, facult
 y\, and the community to engage with collections\, IU-generated creative a
 ctivity\, and community projects in meaningful and innovative ways.
GEO:39.1709246;-86.527549
LOCATION:McCalla Center\, McCalla Center\, 525 E. 9th Street\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/treasures-of-the-silk-road/5960
 4/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
