BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gallery Walk:Opening Reception:"Littles" by Wyatt LeGrand
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261106T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261106T200000
UID:52d7ffeb8ef3e107fa39286778581dcc@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Juniper's Regional Artist\, Wyatt LeGrand (Bloomfield\, IN)\, 
 has created a multi-media exhibit of small\, affordable works of art for y
 our enjoyment and for the holiday season ahead! These original works are a
 ll framed and ready to hang. They will make a thoughtful one-of-a-kind gif
 t or a special treat for yourself. \nArtists Statement:\n"On the north sid
 e of my house\, from chives to chimney\, no less than one hundred and fift
 y spiders spend every evening weaving webs. Of this multiples of eight-leg
 ged clutter\, one particularly plump and colorful specimen garners my admi
 ration each night while I take the dog out for a pee. It’s an eye level 
 sort of spider\, or thereabouts\, unbothered under the porch light by the 
 hinge of the screen door. Its web always swings from the glass and brass f
 ixture to the vinyl siding to the soffit and back again…a scalene shape 
 habitually and boldly outlined by nine o’clock…a routine. And by ten t
 hirty\, cross-contours zig-zag to the center where the spider waits for me
  to admire it and its latest masterpiece before I turn in. The patterns gl
 immer in the glow of my patio decorations\, reminding me of a Navajo rug
 —exercises in symmetry and repetition. I initially admired these creatio
 ns as a naturalist\, an inadvertent fan due to bedtimes and my curiosity\,
  but I soon began studying them like a devoted scientist. And now\, weeks 
 later\, after examining a substantial body of work\, every delicate silk t
 apestry gets my two cents as an art critic. The compositions are changing.
  I notice voids between trapezoidal shapes growing larger each time\, maki
 ng the complexities seem more complex\, the breaks seemingly more purposef
 ul\, and the beauty undoubtedly more beautiful than the previous night’s
  rendition. The spider is getting better at making its webs. And when the 
 dew and the wind unwind what was woven\, sometime after I’ve woke but be
 fore I’ve cared to notice\, the spider begins again. Tomorrow’s versio
 n will surely be its best\, as will the next one after that and so on unti
 l there is no more spider. But in case you didn’t know already\, the spi
 der doesn’t care about any of this. It’s only hunting.\nSometimes I’
 m a spider\, too. I’ve spun many webs\, but I’m never practicing web-m
 aking. Rather\, I just keep spinning and spinning because that’s what sp
 iders do. The web I’m working on is all I care about until I start anoth
 er web tomorrow. A tangled mess\, with me in the middle\, waiting for the 
 dog to go pee." -Wyatt LeGrand\nWyatt LeGrand is a painter and teacher fro
 m Bloomfield\, Indiana. \ndwBrykalski (a.k.a. David Wierhake)\nwith Specia
 l Guest Otis Emery (student at Fairview Elementary School)\nDavid and spec
 ial guest Emery\, will share keyboard tunes for the opening reception for 
 "Littles" small artworks by Wyatt LeGrand.
GEO:39.1664355;-86.5405013
LOCATION:Juniper Art Gallery\, 615 W. Kirkwood Ave.\, Bloomington\, IN 474
 04
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/gallery-walkopening-receptionli
 ttles-by-wyatt-legrand/60167/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
