BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journal for Audiovisual Ethmusicology Shorts Program | Additional 
 Films and Guests
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261106T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261106T170000
UID:18f5f2665fa8d802c8fb55a31260a1d9@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:This screening is a public rental presented by the Journal for
  Audiovisual Ethnomusicology (JAVEM)\, a peer-reviewed journal dedicated t
 o audiovisual scholarship in ethnomusicology and related fields. Together
 \, the three films demonstrate the range of audiovisual ethnomusicology ac
 ross generations: from Hugo Zemp’s pioneering use of film as a tool for 
 musical analysis\, to Richard K. Wolf’s longitudinal and collaborative e
 ngagement with Kota musical and ritual life\, to Matthew Machin-Autenrieth
  and Dario Ranocchiari’s exploration of music\, history\, and cultural m
 emory in contemporary Granada.\nThe Song of Harmonics/Le chant des harmoni
 ques (dir. Hugo Zemp\, 1989\, France): Made with ethnomusicologist and mus
 ician Trần Quang Hải\, Hugo Zemp’s landmark film investigates overto
 ne\, or biphonic\, singing\, a technique in which a single singer produces
  a low fundamental while selectively emphasizing its harmonics to create a
  second audible melody. Moving between instruction\, Mongolian khöömii p
 erformance\, and scientific investigation\, the film uses spectral and phy
 siological imaging to make otherwise invisible aspects of vocal production
  visible. The Song of Harmonics is also an important example of ethnomusic
 ological filmmaking in which audiovisual media function not simply to docu
 ment musical practice\, but as a method of research and analysis. This scr
 eening is presented in tribute to Hugo Zemp (1937–2026)\, a foundational
  figure in visual ethnomusicology\, who passed away on January 3\, 2026. [
 38 min\; documentary\; English and Mongolian]\nResonant Histories: Echoes 
 of Al-Andalus in Granada (dir. Matthew Machin-Autenrieth and Dario Ranocch
 iari\, 2026\, Spain): Resonant Histories traces the complex relationship b
 etween Arab-Andalusian music and flamenco in Granada\, a city where encoun
 ters between North African and Spanish musicians have long shaped musical 
 life. Traveling through the streets of Sacromonte\, closely associated wit
 h flamenco\, and the Albaicín\, Granada’s historic Arab quarter\, the f
 ilmmakers draw on musicians\, musicologists\, historians\, and local enthu
 siasts to explore the ways the city’s past continues to resonate in the 
 present. The film moves from colonial exhibitions and early theatrical exp
 eriments combining Andalusí music and flamenco to Granada’s contemporar
 y music scene\, asking how histories of encounter\, exchange\, and represe
 ntation are remembered—and continually remade—through musical practice
 . [59 min\; documentary\; Spanish with English subtitles]\nPots of Millet
 \, Faces of Gold: Transformation (dir. Richard K. Wolf\, 2025\, India and 
 USA): The first in a series of experimental ethnographic films drawing on 
 Richard K. Wolf’s 35 years of research with the Kota people of South Ind
 ia’s Nilgiri Hills\, Transformation explores a community of fewer than 2
 \,500 people whose history has included disease\, forced migration\, and d
 isruptions in the transmission of traditional knowledge. The film’s titl
 e evokes two poles of Kota ritual life: millet\, central to multiday mortu
 ary ceremonies\, and gold-colored offerings fashioned as faces on the anci
 ent pillars of Kota temples. Conceived as a time capsule addressed to Kota
  people 100 years in the future and narrated in Kota by Wolf’s longtime 
 collaborator Duryodana\, the film explores music alongside transformations
  of materials\, bodies\, and spiritual entities. It moves from pottery and
  musical-instrument making to their roles in mortuary ritual\, combining W
 olf’s footage from across decades with David G. Mandelbaum’s archival 
 film and cylinder recordings from 1938 to create a layered portrait of Kot
 a village life\, historical change\, and ethnographic encounter. [81 min\;
  documentary\; Kota and Tamil with English subtitles]
GEO:39.168585;-86.517361
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 E. 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/journal-for-audiovisual-ethmusi
 cology-shorts-program-additional-films-and-guests/60026/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Local Clay Potters’ Guild 28th Annual Holiday Pottery Show
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261108
